sub('w4.py',[
("The office phone is 336-849-7903, and hours are Monday to Friday, 8:00 am to 5:00 pm.","Call 336-849-7903 for the office, which opens at 8:00 am and closes at 5:00 pm on weekdays."),
("What is Yadkin County like for someone selling a house?","How big is Yadkin County and who lives there?"),
("Yadkin County is a mostly rural county of about 335 square miles of land, with Yadkinville at its center, according to Census","Yadkin County spans roughly 335 square miles of land and counts about 111 people per square mile, according to Census"),
("The July 2025 estimate is 38,325 residents, up 3.0% from the 2020 base, which works out to about 111 people per square mile.","Its 2025 population estimate is 38,325, a 3.0% gain over 2020."),
("Mean commute time is 29.6 minutes, longer than in the larger nearby places.","Workers average a 29.6 minute trip to their jobs."),
("It must be paid from the sale. The lien attaches to the parcel as of the January 1 listing date (<a href=\"@S@GS_105-355.html\">G.S. 105-355</a>), and the county can collect through a foreclosure action in the nature of a mortgage foreclosure under G.S. 105-374, ending in an auction at the courthouse door.","The closing pays it out of the seller's side. Because the lien is dated to the first of the year (<a href=\"@S@GS_105-355.html\">G.S. 105-355</a>), a sale does not wipe it out, and an unpaid parcel can be collected through a courthouse auction."),
("Before listing a house, ask the tax office for a payoff figure, including solid waste fees, and give it to the closing attorney. Land in a rural area may carry more than one tax layer, so request the full parcel record.","Before listing, request a payoff amount that includes solid waste fees, and hand it to the closing attorney. Rural land can carry extra layers, so ask for the whole parcel record."),
("Taxes are due September 1 under <a href=\"@S@GS_105-360.html\">G.S. 105-360</a>, and the county tax page allows a 2% discount for early payment during August. Paying at face value is allowed until the day before January 6, then interest of 2% applies to February 1 and three-fourths of a percent per month beyond.","The county tax page offers a 2% discount for paying in August, ahead of the September 1 due date set by <a href=\"@S@GS_105-360.html\">G.S. 105-360</a>. Interest starts after the grace period that ends the day before January 6."),
("As with every NC county, ownership and value are set as of January 1, so the bill follows the owner of record that day.","The bill follows the person who held title at the start of the year."),
])
sub('w3.py',[
("Forsyth County's real estate page repeats a detail that matters when a house changes hands: bills are prepared in the name of the owner of record on January 1, and a transfer during the year does not release the seller from liability. Taxes are ordinarily prorated at closing, and the county suggests asking your realtor or closing attorney to confirm that happened.","One line on the county's real estate page matters for sellers: the bill carries the name of whoever held title on January 1, and a mid-year transfer leaves the seller liable. Closings normally prorate the tax, and the county suggests checking with your realtor or attorney that this was done."),
("The unpaid","The unpaid") if False else ("The tax follows the parcel. Under","Debt travels with the land. Under"),
("Request a payoff figure from the Tax Office for the parcel.","Obtain the exact owed amount for the parcel from the county."),
("Ask the closing attorney to list it on the settlement statement.","Have the attorney show it on the settlement sheet."),
("Match the closing date to the tax year. A closing in the first half of the year falls after the January 1 date that fixed the owner, so the seller's name is on the current bill even if the buyer takes the house in March.","Line up your closing with the tax year. A spring closing happens after the date that fixed the owner, so the seller's name sits on that year's bill even when the buyer moves in during March."),
("The same logic helps with an inherited or vacant house: the bill still goes to the owner of record, and unpaid tax grows through interest. Appeals, if you want one, should be filed before the last business day of June, so decide on a value challenge before you list.","An inherited or empty house follows the same rule, and unpaid amounts keep growing through interest. If you want to challenge the value, file by the final business day of June, which means deciding before you list."),
("Forsyth County lists three relief programs and allows an owner only one at a time.","Forsyth County names three relief programs, and an owner may use just one."),
("Which Winston-Salem","Which Winston-Salem") if False else ("What is the last day to","What is the last day to") if False else ("It is the last business day of June, per the county's real estate page. The 2026 date was June 30, which has passed, so ask the office for the date in the current year.","The county's real estate page names the last business day in June. In 2026 that was June 30, and it has passed, so ask the office for the date in the year you are filing."),
("What is the last day to appeal a Winston-Salem value?","When does a Winston-Salem appeal window end?"),
("Possibly yes, because the bill is made out to the owner of record on January 1. Taxes are normally prorated between buyer and seller at closing.","Possibly. The bill carries the January 1 owner's name, and the closing normally splits the cost between buyer and seller."),
("Do I owe tax if I sold my house after January 1?","Will I owe tax after a spring sale?"),
])

